===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{m|gem-pro|*nemaną}}, probably from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{m|ine-pro|*nem-||take}}.

Germanic cognates include {{etyl|osx|-}} {{m|osx|niman}}, {{etyl|odt|-}} {{m|odt|neman}} ({{etyl|nl|-}} {{m|nl|nemen}}), {{etyl|goh|-}} {{m|goh|neman}} ({{etyl|de|-}} {{m|de|nehmen}}), {{etyl|non|-}} {{m|non|nema}} ({{etyl|da|-}} {{m|da|nemme}}), {{etyl|got|-}} {{m|got|𐌽𐌹𐌼𐌰𐌽}}. The Indo-European root was also the source of {{etyl|sga|-}} {{m|sga|nem||present}}, {{etyl|lv|-}} {{m|lv|ņemt||to take}}, {{etyl|sq|-}} {{m|sq|nëm||to take, curse}}, {{etyl|grc|-}} {{m|grc|νέμω}}.

